 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds repeated reads and update lag. A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring realtime fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets provide low-latency push delivery without those dependencies, at the cost of operating connection capacity, reliable publication, and reconnect/catch-up behavior. See [the delivery decision record](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md).
